 {"id":"CF-161","title":"P3 - the repo has no release contract: zero published tags, no VERSION/CHANGELOG, no version scheme in specs, and the two local safety tags resolve from no clone of the remote","status":"pending","priority":"P3","complexity":3,"created_at":"2026-11-04T04:09:58Z","updated_at":"2026-11-04T04:09:58Z","detail":{"fingerprint":"8596a33dcb59d9bc4c4a35b1b0a317679687978f97a2b856a4d5fd01df220fae"},"reasoning":"Measured at origin/main 79e9b59 by the releng lane (tick code-factory-duckbrain-releng-2026-11-04-04-04-40): git ls-remote --tags origin returns zero refs; git tag -l lists two safety-* markers that no clone of the remote can see and whose targets are not ancestors of main; no VERSION file, no CHANGELOG, and no version scheme in specs/. A public repo whose nodes pin a harness range and a repo ref has no way to name the factory revision it installs. No other row owns the release surface (board grep for release/tag/version returns only tangential rows), so this is filed rather than re-measured.","acceptance_criteria":"A release contract exists and the tag surface is coherent and PUBLIC at the published tip.\n1. A version scheme is declared by the design authority (specs/RELEASE.md, or an explicit section in docs/RELEASE.md): what a version means, which commits are taggable, and whether the factory releases at all. This is an OWNER DECISION and the closing event must record it.\n2. `git ls-remote --tags origin` at the published tip lists every tag the scheme requires. Measure today: `git ls-remote --tags origin` prints NOTHING (0 published tags) while `git tag -l` in the workdir lists two local-only `safety-*` markers whose targets are not ancestors of main \u2014 `safety-cf-main-strand-20261103` -> 6598ec3, `safety-code-factory-align-20261101` -> e270fb3. Prove the same from a bare clone: `rm -rf /tmp/releasecheck.git; git clone --bare git@github.com:coding-hermes/code-factory.git /tmp/releasecheck.git; git -C /tmp/releasecheck.git tag -l` prints nothing. Either publish both markers with a documented purpose or drop them, and say which in the contract.\n3. A gate arm proves the contract instead of describing it: `bash tests/selftest.sh` carries a section that FAILS on a named row when the contract file is absent, and FAILS on a named row for a fixture carrying a local-only tag that does not resolve from the published ref. Prove the arm red first (fixture with a local-only tag) and green after.\n4. Either a `VERSION` / `CHANGELOG` exists and matches the newest published tag, or the contract states in so many words that the factory is consumed by git ref only. \"No version, no tags, no statement\" is the state this row is filed against.\nRe-runnable evidence: sections 2 and 3 above are single commands; quote their literal output in the closing event.","probe":null}
 {"id":"CF-162","title":"P2 \u2014 verify-node.sh certifies a node that cannot be dispatched: no leg reads or probes the node's bus endpoint, so VERIFY: PASS coexists with a manifest whose bus.base is the unreachable example default","status":"pending","priority":"P2","complexity":3,"created_at":"2026-11-04T04:20:51Z","updated_at":"2026-11-04T04:20:51Z","detail":{"fingerprint":"9c4b55d2f389c0de8c069ab3b497817adc7db54681d449748ff6393e28a1a53c"},"reasoning":"Measured by the code-factory readme-QA lane at origin/main b9a7923 (2026-11-04), in a worktree of the published tip, with every fixture answer staged so only the scripts' own checks decide the outcome.\n\n(a) A full green `project-node.sh --apply` (fixture harness adapter that passes, a local bare repo, a 200-answering curl, a mode-600 bus identity, floor 1 GiB) prints `PASS  crier_url \u2014 <url>` from layer 00 and then writes `$HOME/.node/node.json` with `bus.base` read from `CRIER_BASE` \u2014 an environment variable no documented step exports \u2014 falling back to the built-in example `http://crier.example:8767`. On the documented path (CRIER_URL exported, nothing else) the manifest therefore records a bus endpoint the run never validated and that resolves nowhere. Exporting `CRIER_BASE` alongside `CRIER_URL` makes the manifest record the validated value, which is the control arm proving the writer reads the wrong variable.\n\n(b) `scripts/verify-node.sh` has five legs \u2014 agent alive, node IP, gateway 200 with `auth_required:true`, unauthenticated request refused, optional artifact digest \u2014 and NO leg that reads the node's bus identity or probes the bus endpoint. Run against stub `bunker` and stub `curl` whose every answer is a PASS-arm answer, it prints `VERIFY: PASS`, exit 0, and its transcript contains zero occurrences of `crier` or `bus`, while the node's manifest names `http://crier.example:8767`. A node can therefore hold a passing certification and be unable to receive a single task.\n\n(c) `scripts/node-create.sh` prints `NODE CREATED` only after verify-node.sh reported every leg PASS (the CF-077 consumer rule), so the composite certification inherits the missing leg: the create path can announce a node the bus will never reach.\n\nThis is not CF-068 (that row is about file existence proving bus identity inside project-node.sh), not CF-078 (that row is about which variable the manifest writer reads), not CF-152 (fixed `/tmp/node-status.json` path), not CF-080 (cloning after the bus layer failed), and not CF-126 (forwarding CRIER_URL into the node). The gap filed here is that the CERTIFICATION has no bus leg at all, so no consumer of verify-node.sh can distinguish a dispatched node from a decorative one.\n\nFiled by: code-factory-readme-qa-2026-11-04-04-00-08.","acceptance_criteria":"1. RED FIRST, and the transcript is part of the deliverable: a numbered section in tests/selftest.sh stages a stub `bunker` and a stub `curl` on PATH plus a node manifest whose `bus.base` answers nothing, runs scripts/verify-node.sh, and asserts the bus leg. On the PRE-FIX scripts/verify-node.sh the section must be shown failing on the measured behaviour \u2014 `VERIFY: PASS`, exit 0, zero `crier`/`bus` mentions in the transcript \u2014 and passing after the fix. Paste both runs and both exit codes into the evidence artifact.\n\n2. A bus leg exists and fails closed: verify-node.sh reads the node's manifest through `bunker exec` and probes the documented bus health route (`<bus.base>/health`) from the control host. Any answer other than 2xx, including timeout and `000`, prints a FAIL naming the measured code, e.g. `FAIL  bus_unreachable \u2014 http://crier.example:8767 -> 000 (no answer)`, and the run exits non-zero. An inconclusive answer must never be a WARN or a skip.\n\n3. The passing arm is a probe, not a shape check: with a stub answering 200 for `<bus.base>/health`, the same section prints `PASS  bus_reachable \u2014 <base> -> 200` and the summary stays `VERIFY: PASS`, exit 0. The stub must answer only the health route, so a leg that probes nothing (or only greps the manifest) cannot print this row.\n\n4. A missing or unreadable manifest is a FAIL, not a skip: when `$HOME/.node/node.json` is absent on the node, the run prints `FAIL  bus_manifest \u2014 absent` naming the path it looked for and exits non-zero; a node without a manifest cannot be certified.\n\n5. No consumer may print success past a red bus leg: scripts/node-create.sh must not print `NODE CREATED` when verify-node.sh's bus leg FAILed \u2014 a fixture arm proves `NODE INCOMPLETE`, exit 1, with the bus FAIL row visible in the transcript.\n\n6. The docs and the code agree on the variable: whichever environment value the bus leg and the manifest writer use (`CRIER_URL`, `CRIER_BASE`, or a manifest field), the README quick start and the node section of docs/OPERATIONS.md name it with its default, and that citation matches the script's actual read. Either the writer stops reading an undocumented variable, or the documented variable becomes the one it reads.\n\n7. Gates at the closing commit: `bash tests/selftest.sh` prints `SELFTEST: PASS`; `scripts/boardctl validate` prints `RESULT: OK`; `ruff check .` and `ruff format --check .` exit 0; the GitReins verdict names the implementation commit and `git ls-remote origin refs/heads/main` equals the local HEAD after push.\n\nRe-runnable evidence: sections 2-5 are each a single command against the fixture tree, so the closing event must quote the literal FAIL and PASS rows with their exit codes.","probe":null}
 {"id":"CF-163","title":"P1 — the PUBLIC repo publishes an unrelated `master` lineage: 772 commits of internal fleet material (monitoring boards, ~/.hermes scripts, worktrees, h3 foreman trees) are fetchable from every clone, and 547 gitleaks hits live on 64 of its commits","status":"pending","priority":"P1","complexity":3,"created_at":"2026-11-04T04:38:31Z","updated_at":"2026-11-04T04:38:31Z","detail":{"fingerprint":"f53af6dff2ada36f8d2ec965f1dea39bb14e0cbd5bbb61643c4b35f7a6915845"},"reasoning":"Measured by the releng lane (tick code-factory-duckbrain-releng-2026-11-04-04-04-40, resumed execution) from a fresh clone of the PUBLIC remote git@github.com:coding-hermes/code-factory.git at published main c4fe94f (measurement 2026-11-04T04:19Z, host clock 2026-11-03T23:19-05:00).\n\nThe public repo carries a second, unrelated published branch `master` at 4ead64f: 772 commits, `git merge-base --is-ancestor origin/master origin/main` = false (no shared history with main's 321 commits). Its tip tree is the pre-split monorepo/home-directory content, not this project: `.hermes/`, `monitoring/`, `crier-fleet/`, `crier-interface/`, `get-h3/`, `gitreins-poc/`, `h3-sdk-go-foreman/`, `h3-sdk-python-foreman/`, `sdk-typescript/`, `tmp/`, `worktrees/`, `.gitreins/`.\n\nSecret-scan arm over ALL published refs (`gitleaks detect -c .gitleaks.toml --redact --log-opts=\"--all\"`) scans 1152 commits / 57.39 MB and reports 547 findings in 64 commits. Ancestry classification: 0 of those 64 commits are ancestors of main; all 64 are on `master`. The main-only arm (`--log-opts=\"origin/main\"`, 300 commits) reports `no leaks found`, which is why a main-scoped scan reads as a clean public posture. All 7 flagged files still exist at master's tip, so a stranger cloning the repo today can read them.\n\nFinding classes: 524 of the 547 hits are in `monitoring/.coding-hermes/board/tasks.jsonl` (rule sk-api-key / generic-api-key) and 1 more in `monitoring/.coding-hermes/evidence/monitoring-foreman-2026-11-02-05-53-13-diskfull.json` — these are internal FLEET OPERATIONAL RECORDS (tick summaries, host names such as karahermes/bunker-mvp, incident detail), not this project's code. The remainder are the secret scanner's own detection patterns and fixtures (`.hermes/scripts/scan-git-secrets.py`, two `.bak` copies, `test_secret_scrub.py`, `gitreins-poc/sandbox/test_review_sample.py`) carrying `[REDACTED]`/fake markers, and are false positives of their own rules.\n\nNo live credential is claimed: GitHub's secret scanning on this public repo reports 0 open alerts. The defect is the published internal material itself plus the fact that the repo's own scanner arm can read green while a whole unrelated lineage is public.\n\n`master` is not historical residue — it is an ACTIVE publication channel for another project's automated jobs. Day histogram of `git log --format=%cd --date=short origin/master`: 2026-10-29..11-02 → 19/17/15/18 commits, 2026-11-03 → 5 commits. Tip and recent subjects: `qa-cron: 2026-11-03 findings (1 new, 0 deduped)` (08:42-0500, appends one line to `monitoring/.coding-hermes/board/tasks.jsonl`), `monitoring-foreman-2026-11-03-10-55-42: recover board clobbered by e93eac07e9`, monitoring tick rows naming `bunker-mvp` and `karahermes` disk/alert detail. Each of those commits widens the public exposure, and `recover board clobbered` shows the branch is force-rewritten under no protection (CF-120).\n\nOwner decision required: retire the branch (delete `master`) or re-point it at main's history, stop the pusher, and record the residual — GitHub keeps commits reachable by SHA after a branch deletion, so a purge needs the owner's call.","acceptance_criteria":"The public ref surface and the secret-scan arm agree, at the published tip, that no unrelated lineage is published.\n\n0. Reproduce on the current tree: the row's `probe` command prints the published branch list, the count of `refs/heads/master` (expected 1 today, 0 after the fix) and the master tip's top-level tree entries; it exits 0.\n\n1. Branch retired: from a fresh mirror clone of https://github.com/coding-hermes/code-factory.git, `git for-each-ref --format='%(refname)' 'refs/heads/*'` lists only branches descending from main — no `refs/heads/master` that is not an ancestor of main (`git merge-base --is-ancestor refs/heads/master refs/heads/main` exits 0). Command: `git clone -q --mirror https://github.com/coding-hermes/code-factory.git \"$(mktemp -d)/r.git\" && git -C \"$_\" for-each-ref --format='%(refname)' 'refs/heads/*'`.\n\n2. Whole-ref scan is clean or allowlisted: in the same mirror clone, `gitleaks detect -c .gitleaks.toml --redact --log-opts=\"--all\"` reports `no leaks found`, or every remaining finding is matched by an explicit path or rule entry in `.gitleaks.toml` (no `--all`-only findings are left unclassified in the evidence file).\n\n3. Scan scope is recorded, not implied: the releng/posture arm writes the exact `--log-opts` value it used beside its clean/leak claim in the lane evidence file, so a main-scoped scan can never again read as a whole-repo clean posture (`grep -n 'log-opts' docs/audits/code-factory-releng-*.md` shows one line per claim).\n\n4. Internal material is not re-published: a fresh clone's `master`-lineage files `monitoring/.coding-hermes/board/tasks.jsonl`, `monitoring/.coding-hermes/evidence/*.json` and `.hermes/` are unreachable from any published ref (`git -C \"$clone\" cat-file -e refs/heads/master:monitoring/.coding-hermes/board/tasks.jsonl` exits non-zero once the branch is retired, and no other published ref carries the path: `git for-each-ref --contains` census in the evidence file).\n\n5. Residual disclosed: the evidence file states whether the deleted branch's commits remain reachable by SHA (GitHub keeps them; only an owner-requested purge removes them) and what the owner decided.","probe":"tmp=$(mktemp -d) && git clone -q --mirror https://github.com/coding-hermes/code-factory.git \"$tmp/r.git\" && printf 'branches=%s\\n' \"$(git -C \"$tmp/r.git\" for-each-ref --format='%(refname)' 'refs/heads/*' | tr '\\n' ' ')\" && printf 'master_refs=%s\\n' \"$(git -C \"$tmp/r.git\" for-each-ref --format='%(refname)' 'refs/heads/master' | wc -l)\" && printf 'master_tip=%s\\n' \"$(git -C \"$tmp/r.git\" log -1 --format='%h %s' refs/heads/master)\" && printf 'master_ancestor_of_main=%s\\n' \"$(git -C \"$tmp/r.git\" merge-base --is-ancestor refs/heads/master refs/heads/main && echo yes || echo no)\" && printf 'master_top_tree=%s\\n' \"$(git -C \"$tmp/r.git\" ls-tree --name-only refs/heads/master | tr '\\n' ' ')\""}
